<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>Relations (Equivalence Relations and Equivalence Classes)</title>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>What's a relation?</title>

            <p>
                Here is the most general definition of relation (which is on
                page 393 of your textbook):
            </p>

            <definition>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        A <term>relation from <m>A</m> to <m>B</m></term> is a
                        subset <m>\sim</m> of <m>A\times B</m>.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        A <term>relation on <m>A</m></term> is a subset
                        <m>\sim</m> of <m>A\times A</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </definition>

            <p>
                Recall the
                <term>Cartesian product of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m></term> is
                denoted <m>A\times B</m> and means the ordered pairs where the
                first element is in <m>A</m> and the second element is in
                <m>B</m>.
                E.g.,
                <md>
                    A\times B = \{ (a,b): a\in A \text{ and } b\in B\}.
                </md>
            </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>Representations and Examples</title>

            <p>
                A relation can be written as
            </p>

            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            a <q>rule</q> (typically an if or an if and only if
                            statement),
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            a set of ordered pairs (either in set-builder or
                            roster notation)
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            a directed graph
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>

            <p>
                <term>Example:</term> In the first example in Chapter 9, the
                author defines a relation <m>A = \mathbb{N}</m> where we say
                <m>a\sim b</m> if <m>a\le b</m>.
                Here's 3 different ways to write that relation on
                <m>\mathbb{N}</m>:
            </p>

            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>a\sim b</m> if and only if <m>a\le b</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\{(a,b)\in\mathbb{N}\times\mathbb{N}: a\le b\}</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Notice that it's hard to write the set form in
                            roster notation, we might write something like
                            <md>
                                \{(1,2), (1,3), (1,4),\dots, (2,3), (2,4), (2,5), \dots, (3,4), (3,5), (3,6),\dots \}
                            </md>
                            but even then it might be hard for our reader to see
                            what's going on.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            We can't draw the graph neatly on infinitely many
                            things, but here's one if the relation were on
                            <m>\{1,2,3,4,5\}</m>:
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-relation">
                <caption>
                    A graph of the relation on <m>\{1,2,3,4,5\}</m>
                </caption>

                <image source="images/Relation.png" width="40%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        A graph of the relation on a subset.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <activity xml:id="ch09-act-relations" workspace="1.5in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    For the following examples of relations, give a pair
                    <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> such that <m>x\sim y</m> and a
                    different pair <m>w</m> and <m>z</m> such that
                    <m>w\not\sim z</m>.
                    (Note, the next activity will require you to work on these
                    same relations, so leave space!)
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\sim = \{(x,y) \in \mathbb{R}\times \mathbb{R}: x&lt;y\}</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\sim</m> on <m>\mathbb{Z}</m> such that
                        <m>x\sim y</m> if and only if <m>x\mid y</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>R</m> on <m>\mathbb{Z}</m> such that <m>xR y</m> if
                        and only if <m>x\equiv y\pmod{4}</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\sim</m> from <m>\{1,2,3\}</m> to
                        <m>\mathcal{P}(\{1,2,3\}</m> such that <m>x\sim A</m> if
                        <m>x\in A</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>A = \{1,2,3,4,5,6\}</m> and
                        <m>R = \{ (x,y) \in A\times A: x+y\text{ is even } \}.</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </activity>
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>More Definitions!</title>

            <p>
                Some relations on a set <m>A</m> are special because they act
                like <q>=</q> (and, IMHO, = is one of the most important symbols
                in all of math!).
                One of the reasons <q>=</q> is so important, is that it has all
                of the properties listed in the next definition.
            </p>

            <definition>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        We say a relation <m>\sim </m> from <m>A</m> to <m>B</m>
                        is
                        <ul>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <term>Reflexive:</term> if for all
                                    <m>a\in A</m>, <m>a\sim a</m>
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <term>Symmetric:</term> if for all
                                    <m>a,b\in A</m>, if <m>a\sim b</m> then
                                    <m>b\sim a</m>.
                                </p>
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <term>Transitive:</term> if for all
                                    <m>a,b,c \in A</m>, if <m>a\sim b</m> and
                                    <m>b\sim c</m> then <m>a\sim c</m>.
                                </p>
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        If all three of the properties hold, we call the
                        relation an <term>equivalence relation</term>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </definition>

            <activity workspace=".5in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        For each of the relations defined in
                        <xref ref="ch09-act-relations"/>, which of the 3
                        properties reflexive, symmetric, transitive, hold?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </activity>

            <activity>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        Complete the following with your team:
                    </p>
                </introduction>

                <task workspace="1in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Let <m>A = \{1,2,3\}</m> and define <m>\sim</m> on
                            <m>A</m> by <m>\sim = \{ (1,1), (1,2), (3,1)\}</m>.
                            Is <m>R</m> reflexive?
                            Symmetric?
                            Transitive?
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>

                <task workspace="1in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Let <m>A = \{1,2,3\}</m>.
                            Find a relation <m>R</m> on <m>A</m> such that
                            <m>R</m> is symmetric, but not transitive.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>

                <task workspace="1in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Let <m>A =  \{1,2,3\}</m>.
                            Find a relation <m>R</m> on <m>A</m> such that
                            <m>R</m> is symmetric and transitive, but not
                            reflexive.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>
</handout>